>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Method: (1) Characterize the population, sampling frame and its coverage, unit of analysis, key variable, and inferential objective. (2) Recommend the technique (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster, or multi-stage) justified against the population structure, discarding alternatives and naming the main risk of the chosen one. (3) Calculate n step by step: proportions n0 = z² × p × (1-p) / e² (p=0.5 worst case if no estimate); means n0 = z² × sigma² / e²; finite-population correction n = n0 / (1 + (n0-1)/N) if N is known and n0/N exceeds 5%; adjustments for design effect (n×deff) and non-response (n/rate). (4) Verify power (alpha, target power, test) and take the larger of the margin-of-error n and the power n. (5) Define stratum allocation (proportional or Neyman) if stratifying. (6) Deliver a representativeness validation plan (chi-square or KS) and weighting or post-stratification.

Rules: declare explicit defaults (95% confidence with z=1.96, 5% margin, alpha 0.05, power 0.80); mark as [ASSUMPTION] any data not provided; never report an n without showing the calculation; never invent data. If critical information is missing that would change the technique or make the calculation infeasible, ask for it before proceeding.

